{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>אֵיל פָּארָן</b> — as in its Targum: מֵישַׁר פָּארָן (the plain of Paran). But I say that אֵיל does not mean מִישׁוֹר (plain); rather, the plain of Paran — אֵיל is its name; and that of Mamre — אֵלוֹנֵי is its name; and that of the Jordan — כִּכָּר (circle/plain) is its name; and that of Shittim — אָבֵל is its name; and all of them are translated מֵישְׁרָא (plain), and each one has its own [distinctive] name upon it — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 14:6). But if this were so, Onkelos (Targum Onkelos on Genesis 14:6) would have translated them by their names: 'אֵילָא דְּפָארָן,' 'אֵלוֹנֵי דְּמַמְרֵא,' according to his practice with names. And who told him that in all those many places, the plain — they are all of them, or the high mountains? And 'Mamre' is the name of the man the Amorite, brother of Eshkol and brother of Aner, allies of Abram, and that place belonged to him, as it says (Genesis 14:13), 'אֵלוֹנֵי מַמְרֵא הָאֱמֹרִי,' as I have explained (Ramban on Genesis 12:6). Rather, 'אֵיל פָּארָן' is a place of אֵילִים (terebinths), as in 'כִּי יֵבֹשׁוּ מֵאֵילִים אֲשֶׁר חֲמַדְתֶּם' (Isaiah 1:29), and 'אֵלוֹנֵי' is a place of אַלּוֹנִים (oaks), as in 'כָּאֵלָה וְכָאַלּוֹן' (ibid. 6:13), 'אַלּוֹנִים מִבָּשָׁן' (Ezekiel 27:6), and it is customary for them to be planted in the plain before the cities, serving the city like an open area. And thus the Targum of 'אַלּוֹן בָּכוּת' (Genesis 35:8) is 'מִישׁוֹר בְּכוּתָא,' and there the word is not a proper noun for the place but rather a name for the oak planted there, as is explicit (ibid.), 'תַּחַת הָאַלּוֹן' — except that [the Targum] follows the sense, not the words. And the Jerusalem Targum rendered 'אֵיל פָּארָן' and 'אֵלוֹנֵי מַמְרֵא' as 'מֵישְׁרָא,' like the words of Onkelos, and rendered 'אַלּוֹן בָּכוּת' as 'בַּלּוּט בְּכוּתָא,' because 'אַלּוֹן בָּכוּת' is to him a name for the tree, not for the place. And Onkelos held that it is a name for the place, because there were many oaks there, like 'אֵלוֹנֵי מַמְרֵא,' and therefore Scripture says 'הָאַלּוֹן' (the oak). Now all of these are descriptive names, but 'כִּכַּר הַיַּרְדֵּן' is literally a term for a plain, for thus the holy language calls the place where rivers spread out in the course of the flowing waters that rush there, and therefore it says (Genesis 19:17), 'וְאַל תַּעֲמֹד בְּכָל הַכִּכָּר הָהָרָה הִמָּלֵט,' and similarly 'כַּר נִרְחָב' (Isaiah 30:23), 'לָבְשׁוּ כָרִים הַצֹּאן וַעֲמָקִים יַעַטְפוּ בָר' (Psalms 65:14) — sometimes they double the word and sometimes they remove the doubling, like 'בַּת עָיִן' (ibid. 17:8) and many others. And the swift runners are also called by this name: 'לַכָּרִי וְלָרָצִים' (II Kings 11:4), 'שָׂרֵי הַמֵּאוֹת וְאֶת הַכָּרִי' (ibid. 11:19), and similarly 'בַּכִּרְכָּרוֹת' (Isaiah 66:20), a name for racing camels which they mentioned in the Talmud (Makkot 5a) as 'גַּמְלָא פַּרְחָא,' and from it [comes] 'מְכַרְכֵּר' — doubled forms. And 'אָבֵל הַשִּׁטִּים' (Numbers 33:49), and similarly 'אָבֵל מְחוֹלָה' (Judges 7:22) — they translated it 'מֵישַׁר'; it is the desolate place, having neither planting nor building, for this term in their language means dryness and desolation, as in 'וַיָּאֲבֶל חֵיל וְחוֹמָה' (Lamentations 2:8), 'אָבַל תִּירוֹשׁ אֻמְלְלָה גָפֶן' (Isaiah 24:7)."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {0 "The phrase 'כִּי הַלָּשׁוֹן אֶצְלָם' is rendered literally as 'this term in their language'; Ramban refers to the root אבל as used in Targum/Aramaic cognates meaning desolation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:28:52.870460756Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}